Presidential elections were held in [[Serbia]] on 6 May 2012 alongside [[2012 Serbian parliamentary election|parliamentary elections]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.b92.net/info/izbori2012/vesti.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=06&nav_id=606708 |title=A sada – vreme za pregovore |publisher=B92.net |date=6 May 2012 }}</ref> The elections were called following [[President of Serbia|President]] [[Boris Tadić]]'s early resignation in order to coincide with the parliamentary and local elections to be held on the same date. The Speaker of the Parliament, [[Slavica Đukić Dejanović]], took over as the Acting President.<ref>{{cite web|author=Bojana Barlovac |url=http://www.balkaninsight.com/en/article/slavica-djukic-dejanovic-serbia-s-new-acting-president |title=Slavica Djukic-Dejanovic, Serbia's New Acting President |publisher=Balkaninsight.com }}</ref> As no candidate won a majority, a runoff was on 20 May, with incumbent Tadić facing [[Tomislav Nikolić]] of the [[Serbian Progressive Party]].<ref>{{Citation |url=http://www.b92.net/eng/news/politics-article.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=07&nav_id=80126 |title=Nikolić confident of winning in second round |work=B 92 |date=7 May 2012 |access-date=7 May 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120508074037/http://www.b92.net/eng/news/politics-article.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=07&nav_id=80126 |archive-date=8 May 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

According to preliminary results published by [[CeSID]],<ref>{{cite web|title=Preliminarni rezultati izbora|url=http://www.cesid.org/lt/|publisher=CeSID|language=sr|access-date=20 May 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120517061323/http://www.cesid.org/lt|archive-date=17 May 2012|url-status=usurped}}</ref> [[Ipsos]]<ref>{{cite web|title=Uživo: Nikolić predsednik Srbije |url=http://www.b92.net/info/izbori2012/|publisher=B92|language=sr}}</ref> and RIK, [[Tomislav Nikolić]] had beaten his opponent [[Boris Tadić]] to become President of Serbia.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.b92.net/eng/news/politics-article.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=20&nav_id=80348 |title=Serbia's new president addresses reporters |publisher=B92 |access-date=2012-05-22 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120521162845/http://www.b92.net/eng/news/politics-article.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=20&nav_id=80348 |archive-date=21 May 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Official results confirmed this, putting Nikolić at 51% against Tadić's 49%.

==Candidates==
Despite being elected president of Serbia in [[2004 Serbian presidential election|2004]] and [[2008 Serbian presidential election|2008]], [[Boris Tadić]] was allowed to run for president for a third time, despite the 2 term constitutional limit, as the ''Constitutional Law for the Implementation of the Constitution of the Republic of Serbia'' from 2006 declared that the 2008 election would be considered the first election, following the break-up of [[Serbia and Montenegro]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.slobodnaevropa.org/a/da_li_tadic_ima_pravo_na_trecu_kandidaturu/24442072.html |title=Da li Tadić ima pravo na treću kandidaturu |date=2012-01-04 |access-date=2025-07-20 |work=[[Radio slobodna Evropa]] |first=Ognjen |last=Zorić}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.paragraf.rs/propisi/ustavni_zakon_za_sprovodjenje_ustava_republike_srbije.html |title=Ustavni zakon za sprovođenje Ustava Republike Srbije |access-date=2025-07-20}}</ref>

First round of the elections was held on 6 May. Republic Electoral Commission has confirmed twelve candidates. Candidate numbers were decided using a random draw on 20 April.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.b92.net/info/izbori2012/vesti.php?yyyy=2012&mm=04&dd=20&nav_id=602419 |title=Stanković prvi na izbornom listiću |publisher=B92.net |date=20 April 2012 }}</ref>
