About 6.7 million people were eligible to vote for the 12 candidates. The [[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe]] will undertake the organization of voting for the roughly 109,000 Serb voters in Kosovo.  These results include the districts of Kosovo-Metohija autonomous province, which at the same time has elections independent of the Serbian nation.<ref name="aljaz">{{cite web|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/europe/2012/05/20125681052758635.html |title=Polls close in Serbia's general election – Europe |publisher=Al Jazeera}}</ref> Voting stations were open from 7:00 to 20:00<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.b92.net/eng/news/politics-article.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=06&nav_id=80110 |title=Info – Polling stations close across Serbia |publisher=B92 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120509053435/http://www.b92.net/eng/news/politics-article.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=06&nav_id=80110 |archive-date=9 May 2012}}</ref> with no incidents reported across the country. Voter turnout by 18:00 was 46.34% in Belgrade, 48.37% in central Serbia and 47.89% in Vojvodina.<ref>{{cite web|author=Bojana Barlovac |url=http://www.balkaninsight.com/en/article/polls-close-in-serbia-elections |title=Voting Finishes Without Incidents in Serbia |publisher=Balkan Insight }}</ref> The first round resulted in no clear victory for any candidate. With 25% of ballots counted, Boris Tadić was leading with 26.7% over Tomislav Nikolić who had 25.5% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|last=Nielsen |first=Nikolaj |url=http://euobserver.com/15/116162 |title=/ Enlargement / Pro-EU Serb leader fails to get clear victory |publisher=Euobserver.com |date=8 May 2012 }}</ref>

In the second round Nikolić received 51% of the vote to 49% for Tadić. The results were a surprise, as stated by [[Russian media]], based on previous polls.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.b92.net/info/izbori2012/vesti.php?yyyy=2012&mm=05&dd=21&nav_id=611120 |title=Ruski mediji o pobedi Nikolića |publisher=B92.net |date=2012-05-17 |access-date=2012-05-22}}</ref> "This was an electoral earthquake, a totally unexpected result," political analyst [[Slobodan Antonić]] said on Serbia's [[Radio Television of Serbia|RTS]] state television.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.france24.com/en/20120521-nationalist-nikolic-wins-serbian-presidential-elections |title=Nationalist Nikolić wins Serbian presidential elections |publisher=France24.com |access-date=2012-05-22 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120524092721/http://www.france24.com/en/20120521-nationalist-nikolic-wins-serbian-presidential-elections |archive-date=24 May 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

